    dict(title='Cities That Breathe', paras=[
        'Indian cities are growing at a speed few planners imagined. Every year millions of people move from villages to towns in search of work. Roads widen, flyovers rise and open land vanishes under concrete. In the process, many cities have lost the trees, lakes and open spaces that once kept them cool and healthy.',
        'The cost of this loss is now visible. Paved surfaces absorb heat during the day and release it at night, making cities several degrees warmer than the countryside around them – an effect known as the urban heat island. Lakes that once soaked up monsoon rain have been filled in, so streets flood after a single heavy shower.',
        'Some cities are trying to reverse the damage. Communities have revived dried-up lakes by removing encroachments and clearing inlets. Municipal bodies have planted dense mini-forests on small plots, using native species that grow quickly and attract birds and insects. Rooftop gardens and permeable pavements that let water seep into the ground are slowly becoming more common.',
        'Citizens play a central role. Residents\' associations that adopt a park or a lake often keep it cleaner than any official agency. Children who grow up planting and caring for trees are more likely to defend them as adults. Planners increasingly speak of "green infrastructure" – the idea that trees and wetlands are as essential to a city as roads and pipes.',
        'There are no quick fixes. A tree planted today will take years to give real shade. But the alternative – hotter summers, worse floods and poorer health – is far more costly. A city that breathes, the writer argues, is not a luxury but a necessity.'],
        qs=[(0, 'Why are Indian cities growing so fast?', 'Millions move from villages to towns in search of work.', None),
            (0, 'What have many cities lost in the process of growth?', 'Trees, lakes and open spaces', ['Their names', 'Their roads', 'Their schools']),
            (1, 'What is the "urban heat island" effect?', 'Paved surfaces absorb heat by day and release it at night, so cities are warmer than the countryside.', None),
            (1, 'Why do streets now flood after heavy rain?', 'Lakes that absorbed rain have been filled in', ['Too many trees', 'Rivers have dried', 'Roads are too wide']),
            (2, 'How have communities revived lakes?', 'By removing encroachments and clearing inlets.', None),
            (2, 'Why are native species used in mini-forests?', 'They grow quickly and attract birds and insects', ['They are imported', 'They need no water ever', 'They are cheaper to cut']),
            (2, 'What are permeable pavements?', 'Pavements that let water seep into the ground.', None),
            (3, 'What does "green infrastructure" mean?', 'The idea that trees and wetlands are as essential to a city as roads and pipes.', None),
            (3, 'Who often keeps parks cleaner than official agencies?', 'Residents\' associations', ['Tourists', 'Builders', 'Factory owners']),
            (4, 'Why are there "no quick fixes"?', 'Trees take years to grow enough to give real shade.', None),
            (2, 'Which word in paragraph 3 means "illegal occupation of land"?', 'encroachments', ['inlets', 'species', 'pavements']),
            (4, 'The writer believes that a green city is …', 'a necessity', ['a luxury', 'impossible', 'unimportant']),
            (3, 'Why are children who plant trees likely to defend them as adults?', 'Caring for trees from childhood creates attachment and responsibility.', None)]),
    dict(title='Learning a Second Language', paras=[
        'Most Indians grow up hearing more than one language. A child in Bengaluru may speak Kannada with friends, Tamil at home and English at school, switching between them without a second thought. For much of the world, where people speak only one language, this would seem remarkable. In India, it is ordinary.',
        'Researchers have long studied what this does to the brain. Many studies suggest that people who regularly use two or more languages are better at focusing attention and switching between tasks. Because they must constantly choose which language to use, their brains get regular practice in control and flexibility.',
        'There are social benefits as well. A second language opens the door to another culture – its songs, jokes and ways of seeing the world. People who speak the language of their neighbours are more likely to understand and trust them. In a diverse country, this can be a quiet force for unity.',
        'Yet many young people are losing their mother tongues. Urban parents, eager for their children to master English, sometimes stop speaking their own language at home. Within a generation, a family can lose a language that its ancestors spoke for centuries, and with it a store of stories, proverbs and knowledge.',
        'Experts argue that the choice need not be either-or. Children can learn English well while keeping their mother tongue strong, especially if they are encouraged to read and write it, not only speak it. A language, they remind us, is not merely a tool for jobs. It is a home for memory.'],
        qs=[(0, 'How many languages might a child in Bengaluru speak, according to the passage?', 'Three – Kannada, Tamil and English', ['One', 'Two', 'Five']),
            (0, 'Why does the writer call multilingualism "ordinary" in India?', 'Most Indians grow up hearing and using more than one language.', None),
            (1, 'What mental advantage do multilingual people have, according to studies?', 'They are better at focusing attention and switching between tasks.', None),
            (1, 'Why do bilinguals\' brains get "regular practice in control"?', 'They must constantly choose which language to use', ['They sleep more', 'They read less', 'They avoid speaking']),
            (2, 'Name one social benefit of learning a second language.', 'Opens a door to another culture / helps understand and trust neighbours / promotes unity.', None),
            (2, 'In a diverse country, a shared language can be …', 'a quiet force for unity', ['a cause of war', 'useless', 'a barrier']),
            (3, 'Why are some urban children losing their mother tongues?', 'Parents stop speaking it at home in their eagerness for children to master English.', None),
            (3, 'What is lost along with a language?', 'Stories, proverbs and knowledge', ['Money', 'Land', 'Schools']),
            (4, 'How can children keep their mother tongue strong?', 'By reading and writing it, not only speaking it.', None),
            (4, 'What does "It is a home for memory" mean?', 'A language holds a community\'s history, culture and memories.', None),
            (1, 'Which word in paragraph 2 means "the ability to change or adapt easily"?', 'flexibility', ['attention', 'control', 'practice']),
            (4, 'The expression "either-or" in paragraph 5 suggests …', 'having to choose only one option', ['choosing both', 'refusing both', 'a mathematical formula']),
            (3, 'Which earlier lesson in Flamingo shares the concern of paragraph 4?', '"The Last Lesson" – the value of one\'s mother tongue.', None)]),
    dict(title='The Return of Millets', paras=[
        'For thousands of years, millets such as jowar, bajra and ragi fed much of India. They grew on dry land with little water and needed few chemicals. But after the Green Revolution of the 1960s, rice and wheat spread across the country, supported by irrigation and government purchase. Millets came to be seen as "poor people\'s food" and their cultivation fell sharply.',
        'Today, millets are making a comeback. Nutrition experts point out that they are rich in fibre, iron and calcium, and release sugar slowly into the blood, which makes them valuable in a country with rising rates of diabetes. The United Nations declared 2023 the International Year of Millets, following a proposal by India.',
        'Millets are also climate-friendly. As rainfall becomes less predictable, crops that survive drought matter more. Millets need far less water than rice and can grow in poor soil. For farmers in dry regions, they offer security when other crops fail.',
        'Challenges remain. Many urban consumers do not know how to cook millets, and processing them is harder than polishing rice. Prices for farmers are often low because there are few buyers. Some state governments now include millets in school meals and public distribution, creating a steady demand.',
        'Chefs and food start-ups have helped too, turning ragi and jowar into biscuits, pasta and breakfast cereals. Whether millets will regain their old place on Indian plates remains to be seen. But for the first time in decades, the humble grain is being talked about with respect.'],
        qs=[(0, 'Name two millets mentioned in the passage.', 'Jowar, bajra, ragi (any two).', None),
            (0, 'Why did millet cultivation fall after the 1960s?', 'Rice and wheat spread with irrigation and government purchase, and millets were seen as "poor people\'s food".', None),
            (1, 'Why are millets valuable for people with diabetes?', 'They release sugar slowly into the blood', ['They contain no fibre', 'They are very sweet', 'They are expensive']),
            (1, 'Which year was declared the International Year of Millets?', '2023', ['2013', '2020', '2030']),
            (2, 'Why are millets called "climate-friendly"?', 'They survive drought, need far less water than rice and grow in poor soil.', None),
            (2, 'For farmers in dry regions, millets offer …', 'security when other crops fail', ['great luxury', 'no benefit', 'only losses']),
            (3, 'Give two challenges in reviving millets.', 'Consumers do not know how to cook them; processing is harder; low prices; few buyers (any two).', None),
            (3, 'How are some state governments creating demand?', 'By including millets in school meals and public distribution.', None),
            (4, 'How have food start-ups helped?', 'By making biscuits, pasta and cereals from millets', ['By banning millets', 'By exporting all millets', 'By growing wheat']),
            (4, 'What does "the humble grain is being talked about with respect" suggest?', 'Attitudes are changing; millets are now valued rather than looked down upon.', None),
            (1, 'Which word in paragraph 2 means "returning to a former popular position"?', 'comeback', ['proposal', 'nutrition', 'rates']),
            (0, 'The phrase "Green Revolution" refers to …', 'the large increase in food production in the 1960s', ['a forest protest', 'a political party', 'a painting style']),
            (4, 'The writer\'s tone in the last paragraph is …', 'cautiously hopeful', ['bitter', 'despairing', 'sarcastic'])]),
    dict(title='The Art of Listening', paras=[
        'We are taught to read, write and speak, but rarely to listen. Yet listening may be the most used and least practised of all communication skills. Studies of office workers suggest that they spend nearly half their working day listening – in meetings, on calls, in conversations – while remembering only a fraction of what they hear.',
        'True listening is more than hearing. Hearing is a physical process; listening is an act of attention. It means setting aside one\'s own thoughts, resisting the urge to interrupt and trying to understand not only the words but also the feelings behind them. Psychologists call this "active listening".',
        'Good listeners use simple techniques. They maintain eye contact, nod to show they are following, and ask questions that invite the speaker to say more. They sometimes repeat what they have heard in their own words to check that they have understood correctly. Above all, they wait until the speaker has finished before forming a reply.',
        'The rewards are considerable. People who feel heard are more likely to trust, cooperate and share honestly. In families, listening can prevent small misunderstandings from growing into quarrels. In classrooms, teachers who listen to students\' questions discover what they truly find difficult. Even doctors make better diagnoses when they let patients finish describing their symptoms.',
        'Modern life works against listening. Phones buzz, notifications pull attention away, and many people listen only long enough to prepare their own answer. Learning to listen well, the writer suggests, is a quiet kind of courage: the courage to be silent and let another person matter.'],
        qs=[(0, 'How much of the working day do office workers spend listening, according to studies?', 'Nearly half', ['A tenth', 'All of it', 'None of it']),
            (0, 'Why is listening called the "least practised" skill?', 'We are rarely taught to listen, though we use it the most.', None),
            (1, 'How is listening different from hearing?', 'Hearing is physical; listening is an act of attention to words and feelings.', None),
            (1, 'What do psychologists call attentive, understanding listening?', 'Active listening', ['Passive hearing', 'Speed reading', 'Note-making']),
            (2, 'Name two techniques of good listeners.', 'Eye contact, nodding, asking questions, repeating in own words, waiting to reply (any two).', None),
            (2, 'Why do good listeners repeat what they have heard?', 'To check that they have understood correctly', ['To show off', 'To end the talk', 'To change the topic']),
            (3, 'How does listening help in families?', 'It prevents small misunderstandings from growing into quarrels.', None),
            (3, 'When do doctors make better diagnoses?', 'When they let patients finish describing their symptoms', ['When they speak more', 'When they use phones', 'When they hurry']),
            (4, 'How does modern life work against listening?', 'Phones and notifications distract; people listen only to prepare their answer.', None),
            (4, 'Explain "a quiet kind of courage".', 'It takes courage to stay silent, set aside oneself and let another person matter.', None),
            (3, 'Which word in paragraph 4 means "work together"?', 'cooperate', ['trust', 'share', 'discover']),
            (2, 'According to the passage, good listeners form their reply …', 'after the speaker has finished', ['before the speaker begins', 'while interrupting', 'never']),
            (0, 'Suggest a suitable title for the passage other than the one given.', 'e.g. "Hearing Is Not Listening" – any suitable title.', None)]),
